0.187,0.173,0.147,0.175,0.218,0.251,0.206,0.31,0.273,0.395,0.403,0.426,0.43,0.446,0.354,0.585,0.615,0.399,0.554,0.45,0.369,0.424,0.845,0.833,0.682,0.12,0.139,0.1,0.151,0.147,0.202,0.196,0.136,0.141,0.202,0.269,0.216,0.246,0.397,0.525,0.878,0.597,0.574,0.521,0.601,0.699,0.764,0.609,0.375,0.466,0.497,0.448,0.444,0.554,0.489,0.684,0.713,0.532,0.754,0.684,0.283,0.253,0.583,0.305,0.23,0.534,0.499,0.766,0.825,0.525,0.096,0.185,0.244,0.271,0.295,0.271,0.112,0.126,0.169,0.171,0.183,0.157,0.181,0.269,0.344,0.615,1.0,0.658,0.697,0.609,0.707,0.758,0.747,0.678,0.866,0.778,0.513,0.42,0.536,0.603,0.884,0.611,0.525,0.495,0.556,0.715,0.766,0.727,0.574,0.619,0.552,0.686,0.747,0.839,0.727,0.764,0.711,0.664,0.613,0.654,0.872,0.407,0.864,0.658,0.874,0.87,0.452,0.065,0.049,0.022,0.01,0.004]}</script><audio preload="metadata"></audio><figcaption class="sv-listen-credit">US Marine Band, Marine Chamber Orchestra (2019). Public domain, 17 U.S.C. &sect;105.</figcaption></figure><div class="sv-listening"><section class="sv-card sv-card--cover" data-title="The piece"><h2 data-narration-id="n1">A symphony that starts in the wrong key</h2><div class="sv-card-body"><p data-narration-id="n2">Beethoven's Symphony No. 1 in C major is the compulsory set study piece for this course. It was completed and first performed in 1800, and its opening bars<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="0">1</button> already show a composer who respects the rules of the Classical symphony while quietly breaking them.</p></div></section><section class="sv-card" data-title="The opening" data-chapters="c1"><h2>The Adagio molto opening</h2><div class="sv-card-body"><p data-narration-id="n3">The movement begins with a slow <dfn class="term" data-def="An Italian tempo marking meaning very slow.">Adagio molto</dfn> introduction, bars 1 to 12<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="0">1</button>. The very first chord is a <dfn class="term" data-def="A four-note chord built on the fifth degree of a scale, which normally pulls strongly towards the tonic.">dominant seventh</dfn> chord &mdash; but it belongs in F major, not in the symphony's home key of C major. For a symphony written in 1800, opening on a chord that points away from the home key was a striking choice.</p><div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n4" data-revision-tip="No content was provided for this key-fact box, so a specific retrieval task cannot be written without the actual facts, names, or terms it contains."><div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div><p>The Adagio molto introduction touches several harmonic areas without settling. Beethoven delays confirming C major as the <dfn class="term" data-def="The home key or first note of a scale, the point of harmonic rest a piece is centred on.">tonic</dfn>, so that when it finally arrives at the Allegro con brio, it lands with far more force<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="107">2</button>.</p></div></div></section><section class="sv-card" data-title="Arriving in C major" data-chapters="c2"><h2>Arriving in C major</h2><div class="sv-card-body"><p data-narration-id="n6">This was an unusual way to begin a symphony. Most Classical symphonies before 1800 open with a clear statement of the home key, giving the listener firm ground straight away. Beethoven instead makes the listener wait for that certainty, turning the arrival of C major into an event in itself rather than a starting assumption.</p><figure class="sv-card-img"><img src="https://pub-aeb94e100e5a48f4a133be5bf206aecb.r2.dev/music-aqa/cards/beethoven-1803-horneman.jpg" alt="Portrait miniature of Ludwig van Beethoven by Christian Horneman"><figcaption>Beethoven around the time of this symphony. Christian Horneman. Public domain.</figcaption></figure></div></section><section class="sv-card" data-title="Sonata form" data-chapters="c3,c4,c5,c6,c7"><h2 data-narration-id="n7">Sonata form across the Allegro con brio</h2><div class="sv-card-body"><p data-narration-id="n8">The main body of the movement is in <dfn class="term" data-def="A structure in three main stages (exposition, development, recapitulation), often followed by a coda, used to organise a movement around two contrasting musical ideas.">sonata form</dfn>. The <dfn class="term" data-def="The opening section of a sonata-form movement, where the first subject and second subject are introduced.">exposition</dfn> presents two contrasting ideas: a <dfn class="term" data-def="The first main theme presented in the exposition of a sonata-form movement.">first subject</dfn> in the home key, C major, followed by a <dfn class="term" data-def="A contrasting theme introduced later in the exposition, usually in a different key from the first subject.">second subject</dfn> in a related key.<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="143">3</button></p><p data-narration-id="n9">After the exposition comes the <dfn class="term" data-def="The middle section of sonata form, where ideas from the exposition are fragmented, altered and moved through different keys.">development</dfn>, where Beethoven takes fragments of the exposition's themes through new keys and combinations.<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="378">5</button> The <dfn class="term" data-def="The section of sonata form that restates the exposition's themes, now usually staying in the home key.">recapitulation</dfn> then returns to the opening themes, now settled in C major<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="406">6</button>, and a <dfn class="term" data-def="A concluding passage added after the recapitulation to round off a movement.">coda</dfn> rounds the movement off.<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="520">7</button></p></div><div class="sv-stage-strip"><div class="sv-stage"><strong>Exposition <button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="107">2</button></strong><span>First subject, then second subject &mdash; then the whole exposition repeats <button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="232.5">4</button></span></div><div class="sv-stage"><strong>Development <button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="378">5</button></strong><span>Themes fragmented and pushed through new keys</span></div><div class="sv-stage"><strong>Recapitulation <button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="406">6</button></strong><span>The themes return, now in the home key</span></div><div class="sv-stage"><strong>Coda <button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="520">7</button></strong><span>A final closing passage</span></div></div></section><section class="sv-card" data-title="Drive and energy"><h2>Drive and energy</h2><div class="sv-card-body"><p data-narration-id="n10">The Allegro con brio is in <dfn class="term" data-def="A time signature (also written 2/2) with two minim beats in a bar, giving a fast, strong pulse.">alla breve</dfn>, written as 2/2. With only two strong beats per bar instead of four, the music drives forward with real momentum once it gets going.</p><p data-narration-id="n12">Everything about this first subject is built for energy.<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="107">2</button> Short repeated motifs give the theme its drive, sudden <dfn class="term" data-def="A sudden strong accent placed on a single note or chord.">sforzando</dfn> accents jolt the texture, and a rapid crescendo pushes the music forward. After the uncertainty of the introduction, the effect is of tension finally being released.</p></div></section><section class="sv-card" data-title="Orchestration"><h2 data-narration-id="n13">Orchestration: Beethoven and the wind</h2><div class="sv-card-body"><p data-narration-id="n14">The symphony uses a standard late-Classical orchestra: pairs of flutes, oboes, clarinets and bassoons, pairs of horns and trumpets, timpani, and strings. Clarinets were still a relatively new addition to the symphony orchestra in 1800, and Beethoven treats the whole wind section as more than accompaniment.</p><p data-narration-id="n15">Rather than simply doubling the string parts, Beethoven gives the wind instruments their own melodic material<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="143">3</button> and uses them to add weight and colour at key structural moments, such as the arrival of C major at the start of the Allegro.<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="107">2</button> This treatment of the wind section points forward to the far more independent orchestration of his later symphonies.</p></div></section><section class="sv-card" data-title="Beethoven in 1800"><h2 data-narration-id="n16">1800: inheriting Haydn and Mozart, testing the edges</h2><div class="sv-card-body"><p data-narration-id="n17">Beethoven wrote his First Symphony as a former pupil of Haydn and an admirer of Mozart, and the piece uses the four-movement symphonic form, standard orchestra and sonata-form structure they had established. When it was first performed in 1800, this was still recognisably a Classical symphony.</p><p data-narration-id="n18">At the same time, the harmonically unsettled opening<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="0">1</button>, the sudden dynamic contrasts, and the sheer drive of the Allegro con brio<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="107">2</button> show Beethoven testing what a symphony's opening could do. This is why the piece is often described as standing on the boundary between the Classical era and the Romantic era that Beethoven himself would help to create.</p></div></section><section class="sv-card" data-title="Writing about it"><h2 data-narration-id="n20">Writing about the set study piece</h2><div class="sv-card-body"><p data-narration-id="n21">Section B marks come from three things: naming the musical element, giving its correct technical term, and saying where in the music it happens. A general impression of the music, however accurate it feels, earns little on its own.</p><p data-narration-id="n22">Weak: <em>&ldquo;The music sounds exciting and grand, which shows Beethoven was a great composer.&rdquo;</em> This says nothing about the music itself &mdash; no element, no term, no location.</p><p data-narration-id="n23">Strong: <em>&ldquo;The first subject, from around 1:50<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="107">2</button>, creates energy through short repeated motifs and sudden sforzando accents, before a rapid crescendo drives the music forward out of the ambiguous Adagio molto introduction.&rdquo;</em> This names the passage, uses correct terms, and locates it precisely &mdash; the kind of sentence that scores full marks.</p></div></section></div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<p>You now know why Beethoven's Symphony No. 1 opens in the &ldquo;wrong&rdquo; key, how sonata form organises the Allegro con brio, and how he uses the wind section to push beyond Haydn and Mozart. Head to the listening drill for this study piece and practise identifying the introduction and the first subject by ear &mdash; then write your own exam-style sentence about each.</p>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p>When you revise this piece, don&rsquo;t just listen to it &mdash; practise writing one exam-style sentence for each passage. Name the element (dynamics, harmony, texture), give the correct technical term, and state exactly where it happens in the music.</p>

