 {
  "text": "Explain the term sonata form, referring to the structure of the Allegro con brio.",
  "type": "3 marks — Explanation",
  "marks": "1 mark each, up to 3, for correctly identifying the stages in order: exposition (first subject then second subject); development (themes fragmented and moved through new keys); recapitulation (themes return in the home key). Accept mention of the coda as an additional creditable point within the 3-mark cap."
 },
 {
  "text": "State two features of Beethoven's orchestration in this movement that build on the Classical orchestra of Haydn and Mozart.",
  "type": "2 marks — Description",
  "marks": "1 mark each for any two of: use of a full pair of woodwind including clarinets; wind instruments given melodic or thematic material rather than simply doubling the strings; brass and timpani used for weight and emphasis at structural moments."
 },
 {
  "text": "\"Beethoven's Symphony No. 1 shows a composer who respected tradition but was ready to break its rules.\" Discuss this statement with reference to the opening of the movement.",
  "type": "4 marks — Evaluation",
  "marks": "Up to 4 marks for well-evidenced points such as: respects tradition through the four-movement symphonic form, standard Classical-sized orchestra and conventional sonata-form structure inherited from Haydn and Mozart; breaks with convention by opening on a dominant seventh chord that does not belong to the home key, delaying the arrival of C major until the Allegro; this was an unusual and striking choice for a symphony opening in 1800; overall the work shows Beethoven testing the expectations of the genre while still working within its structures."
 }
]

